so  there are there are two a4 isms that are  relevant to thinking about teaching a  seminar I think one is I can't remember  who first said this but that the only  thing a Yale student remembers from a  seminar is what they themselves said in  it and then the other is a story about a  Harvard professor now deceased a  political theorist who used to walk into  her graduate seminars Judith's Laura  some of you may have read some other  books with and her opening remark was  you can talk for as long as you make  sense and then she would interrupt  everybody in the second sentence so  there's a tension between the the the  implications of those two I guess we  should stick with the format suggested  by Miroslav so I'll mainly talk for the  first 15 minutes and then we'll go to  questions but if anyone really is moved  to try and to interrupt me feel free  debate the basic message behind my  comments today might be thought to be  provocative and I think it's a something  somewhat contrarian with respect to the  themes of this course my basic message  is that both globalization and the  importance of faith or religion are  greatly overhyped in the current  discussion overhyped in the popular  literature and in in much of the  academic literature and I'm going to say  a little bit about why I think that in  both cases I'll start off with  globalization now one sense in which  globalization has been overhyped was was  brought out by Doug's commentary on Tom  Friedman's world being flat sometimes I  think arguing about whether or not the  world is flat is a little bit like  arguing over whether or not the number 7  is orange but perhaps it's more the case  that that it's flatter in some respects  and it's more hilly in other respects  and so the question is you know  which of the relevant respects and what  do they really tell us about the ongoing  distribution of power and Welfare and  political stability in the world and how  should we think about that and I think  that Doug makes the very valid point  that most economic activity still goes  on within nation states most goods and  services are produced by national  workforces operating under national  labor labor law regimes and that's all  true despite the increasing mobility of  capital and the declining trade barriers  and so on so I think that that is the  case but I think it's overhyped in a  different sense that focuses more on the  political economy of globalization not  the sheer economics of globalization  although I think more could be said  about the economics of globalization  simple proper and that I think we  actually now are witnessing something of  a retreat from economic globalization  towards regionalization of the world  economy but I'm no economist and so I  won't go there  in any detail now but I think that one  important lesson to draw from the events  of the last year and a half in the  world's political economic order is that  the key responses to the global  financial crisis did not come from  international political institutions or  international economic institutions the  key responses that to the to the global  economic crisis came from national  governments anybody who thinks that  national political systems of  diminishing importance in the world are  missing I think the most important fact  political fact of the of the  International landscape the the the most  important decisions were taken by  sovereign states to some extent in  agreement with one another because they  were all running scale  in the same direction but nonetheless  they were decisions taken principally  aimed at salvaging the the national  economies for which these governments  were responsible and they continue to do  so and I think that as the as the  interests of different national  populations start to diverge as people  retreat from the sort of panic that the  wheels are coming off the entire system  I think you're going to see more  divergence of interest we've already  seen an uptick in in protectionist  activity I think you'll see more of that  and the reassertion of national  sovereignty as a way of combating what's  been going on so although though it's  certainly true that this  interconnectedness of economic problems  of the problems that generated this  crisis and the there there's also a lot  of knock-on effects of the policies  particular governments pursue in  response to it we should we shouldn't  discount the importance of national  political systems in the world  so that means attending to the nature of  national political systems and that I  think inevitably puts democracy on the  table the question whether or not  democratic political systems are the  best response best able to respond to  the problems of globalization and I  think that I'll just I'll just say a few  things about that democratic political  systems are often criticised as being  not very good at dealing with ongoing  problems of political economy  Amartya Sen may made the observation  once that the democracy is a good thing  because on the grounds that we never  have famines in democracies but I think  that the example is is actually sort of  two-sided one because what it  underscores is  that democracies are pretty good at  responding to crises what they're not  very good as it is responding to chronic  problems so yes we don't get famines in  democracy but we do get poverty in  democracies we're not very good at  responding to chronic problems so while  the financial crisis was perceived to be  at crisis levels we got very rapid  response out of the democratic systems  but as it's now as the sense that the  wheels are coming off the world has gone  away it's proving much harder and you  see now the Obama administration can't  even get its resolution trust  legislation through Congress so he's  essentially leaving us with a situation  where we've now publicly admitted that  we have economic institutions that are  too big to fail but we neither have the  nerve to regulate them nor to break them  up so we haven't really resolved the  problem at all so in that sense  democracies might not very be very good  at dealing with with chronic problems  nonetheless those who say so we should  have benevolent authoritarian systems  and you know the system du jour is China  China looks like it did pretty well in  this crisis even in 2008 they're not  going to have double-digit growth but  they are going to have 8% growth in  China this year yes they had  unemployment problems but they're  already beginning to reverse and so I  think we're going to hear a lot of hype  about China in the coming year months  and years as an example of why we should  have authoritarian thinking and this is  this is a point that comes and goes in  in world politics in the 1970s and 1980s  there was a lot of discussion of  democracy as a kind of luxury good that  there would be a democracy tax on growth  that that we we have to pay if we're  going to have democracy and that the the  in those days it was the only  East Asian miracles that were put on the  table as as showing the virtues of  authoritarian capitalism rather than  democratic capitalism and even within  democratic systems countries like Japan  were held out as capable of much better  macroeconomic management and it was  widely noted in connection with this  that Japan had had the same political  party in powers for many decades and so  didn't face the normal challenges of  democratic politics but as a set at the  same time as Japanese success was being  hyped in fact they were piling up one  poor allocation decision after another  because authoritarian systems don't have  good access access to good information  one of the good things markets do is  generate information as a byproduct of  competition and so although everybody  was completely but completely wowed by  the Japanese economy in the 1970s and  and and first half of the 1980s the  chickens finally had came home to roost  in the 1990s when it was seen that they  had been propping up wildly inefficient  industries and were incapable of  competing with the more market-based  competitive systems I suspect that  there's a lot similar going on with  respect to China now that massive miss  allocations of investment are starting  to go on it's very easy in the earliest  stages of industrialization to know what  to do of course the Chinese have to  invest in education and infrastructure  but once you get beyond that  government's trying to pick winners  don't do very well over time and so  betting on authoritarian capitalism is  the wave of the future probably not a  great idea and people should remind  themselves that some of the democracies  have done just as well India for example  a very challenged democracy in many  respects nonetheless is whether this  crisis exceedingly well it's gonna have  6% growth rates this year  Canada has fit weather it well so the  temptation the temptations of  authoritarian globalization since we're  talking the language of temptations  shouldn't be I think  resisted let me say a few things about  faith and all that and religion there's  been a debate at least since Tocqueville  wrote democracy in America about whether  or not religion is good or bad for  democracy and if it is good  what variants of it is good and if it is  bad what variants of it are bad  Tocqueville for example thought  Catholicism was better for democracy  than Protestantism for a variety of  reasons I think the main one was that  once you have a democracy you no longer  have the authoritative allocation of  values by the state and so you need some  other hierarchical order for creating  values in society but in in subsequent  years it actually became more  fashionable to say that Catholicism was  less hospitable to democracy than  Protestantism but then of course we had  the democratization of much of Latin  America so people began revising that  notion that Catholicism was not  particularly hospitable to democracy  since we had many Catholic democracies  and actually of course we go back into  the the combating fascism there were  certainly many Catholics who opposed  fascism in Italy and elsewhere so it  seemed like a more mixed story we've had  a something of a replay of this whole  debate given want given the rise of  Islam as whether is Islam inherently  opposed to democracy some would say yes  some would say no and we have Huntington  wheeling out his  Clash of Civilizations and so on my own  view of this is that there are variants  of all religions that are compatible  with democracy and their variants of all  religions that are incompatible with  democracy because religion is not  particularly important either way with  respect to the survival of democracy  rather two other things are way more  important than religion  the first is economics and the two are  connected the first one is economics and  it is that the by far the best predictor  of whether or not democracies will  survive or is economic basically the the  big finding in the literature is that as  per capita incomes rise toward about  $6,000 a year in 1985 dollars  democracies become less and less likely  to collapse and once per capita income  goes above that level democracy survives  forever once democracies fall below that  level they become more and more  vulnerable and the further they fall  below it the more vulnerable they become  to the point where they very become very  likely to fall apart and be replaced by  something other than another democracy  namely some sort of  arianism so democracy becomes stable if  if you have economic growth and growth  in per capita incomes and elude just  alluding to Doug's slide about  inequality the more equal the less  inequality there is the more that helps  as well as inequality diminishes it  helps as well there's not a lot of  disagreement about that fund about the  existence of that finding there's a lot  of disagreement about why it's true but  the the finding is very robust and  things like culture beliefs institutions  all those things tend to be  a very small part of the variance next  to the economic datum I just gave you  the state-of-the-art is a book on this  if you're interested is a book called  democracy and development with  development and democracy and American  never remember which by Adam Schiff or  ski and variety of co-authors I can give  people more references if they want my  own view about why this is true connects  to the second point I want to make about  what's important in democracy and will  link us back to the religion and that is  as countries become richer you have less  what I would call winner-take-all  politics and when it take all politics  is is bad because it causes people to  try to get monopoly control of the  political system so when Al Gore  discovers that he is not going to become  President of the United States in  January of 2001 there are many other  things Al Gore can do to become happy  and successful and have lots of prestige  and influence in the world he can go on  book to us  he can read lead environmental movements  he can be on the lecture circuit he can  be on corporate boards there are many  other ways for Al Gore to win right but  in most poor countries the political  system is the only game in town so if  you get kicked out of the politics you  essentially are out of luck right and so  politics tends to be winner-take-all  politics I think that the the analog  here with religion that is important is  that it's not so much whether or not  religion exists in the political in in  the political system but rather whether  or not being a member of a particular  religion will  puts you in a position to engage in  winner-take-all politics so it doesn't  matter whether or not there's an  established church so much as whether  it's necessary to be a member of the  established church in order to have  security and prosperity in the society  so we have an established church in the  UK but it's not necessary to be part of  it in order to be successful in that  society on the other hand if you have a  fundamentalist regime it's both  exclusionary and it involves  winner-take-all politics you have to be  part of it it's it's the equivalent of  you know it's the only game in town and  that's what becomes the threat to the  survival of a democratic political order  how are we doing on time yeah okay so  that I probably put enough out there but  the so the the the bottom line is that I  think as I said at the outset that  there's this rather too much hype around  globalization and I think people are  trying to have been trying to load a lot  more significance onto the whole matter  of religion in well politics than is  warranted and I think I'll just close  with the observation that in some way in  one way it's understandable you know if  you go back to the 1950s everybody was  jumping up and down about something  called modernization theory the basic  idea of modernization theory was that  the the Schumpeterian logic of he called  it creative destruction the creative  destruction of markets if you guys read  John Pater yeah which slowly just grind  under traditional practices all forms of  non market rationality would ultimately  succumb to the creative destruction of  capitalism there were earlier Marxist  variants of this as  and and there were many besides  Schumpeter Seymour Martin Lipset and our  own David after all pushed this idea in  the 50s and 60s that it was a kind of  early version of of Tom Friedman in a  way that you know they were going to be  these homogenizing forces and that  turned out not to be right  the politics of modernization are  multifaceted and many sided I think a  lot of the resurgence of identity  politics is in fact a response to the  forces of economic globalization from  the losers because if you're a winner  it's great but if you were a loser  there's not much in globalization for  you why don't I stop there and you can  all tell me why I'm wrong

